// Vignettes, part A (from Chapter One)
const CITIES_A = [
{ id:"dannels-pride", cat:"memory", place:"dannels-pride", title:"Dannel's Pride", body:[
"Dannel's Pride is a ward of crowded rooms inside a giant's ruin, and each room holds a piece of Cyre bought at a price nobody in it could afford. A teacup with its crack mended in gold. A regimental button. A hymnal with a stranger's mother's name on the flyleaf. Families that haven't eaten meat since spring pool their coins for these and set them on the sill where the light is best. Some of the relics are forged. The families who suspect it buy them anyway, because a forgery of Cyre is still more Cyre than anyone else is selling.",
"Ask what Cyre was and each refugee answers with one city. There is the Cyre of lamps, where the streets stayed lit all night by order of a queen. There is the Cyre of orchards, which smelled of pears for a month each autumn. There is the Cyre of one bakery on one corner, the Cyre of a ferry crossing at dusk, the Cyre of a grandmother's kitchen with the shutters half closed. Laid side by side, these cities share no border and no road.",
"The old keep buying. The young have stopped. They take their coins to the back rooms instead, where a militia called Dannel's Wrath keeps a different collection, of scrolls with fire folded into them. Its leader goes by Fury, and he is said to wear a different face for each cell he runs. The young speak of him the way the old speak of the queen who kept the lamps lit. Both collections are called Cyre. The people who tend them pass each other on the stairs in the morning and nod."
]},
{ id:"stone-heads", cat:"memory", place:"stone-heads", title:"The Stone Heads", body:[
"You'll find them in every district, in threes and fours, leaning on walls, teetering on ledges, lying in the gutter with one ear in the mud. They are the heads of giants, broken off statues too large to put back together, each with its own braids and its own expression. Stormreach sells its own graves by the cartload and leaves these alone. There's a curse on them, people say, and in this town a curse is the one price nobody haggles over.",
"The old people will tell you the faces have changed. The one by the fish stalls was smiling in their grandmothers' day; now a lip has drawn back off the teeth. In this account the heads remember the city the giants meant to build and hold it up, year after year, against the one they got. They have watched every knifing in every gutter for two hundred years. They are witnesses no magistrate will ever call, and their faces are the only testimony they give.",
"Watch one for an afternoon and nothing moves. Toward evening a woman comes with a rag and a bowl, cleans the lichen out of its eyes, sets a pickled apple on its lower lip, and goes home. Ask her why and she looks at you the way you would look at a stranger who asked why you wash your own face."
]},
{ id:"hammersmiths", cat:"memory", place:"hammersmiths", title:"Hammersmith's Inn", body:[
"Forgelight is loud, and its people are not. The furnaces roar, the House Deneith yards ring with drill, and the men and women of the district go through it saying as little as they can, as though they were paying by the word. They spend their savings at Hammersmith's. At night its long tables fill with veterans of the Last War, seated without regard to flag. A Karrn sits beside the Brelish sapper who mined his trench. An Aundairian war-wizard pours for the Thrane who once tried to burn her. Two years ago they'd have opened each other's throats on sight. Now there's the toast. Someone stands and names a battle, and everyone who was there, on any side, drinks to that day's dead, and then someone else stands and names another. The war lasted a century. The list hasn't run dry. In the morning they oil their blades and sign on with a Deneith captain to guard a dig in the jungle, and some of them become the next toast. I asked a sergeant what they'd do when the last battle had been drunk to. He looked at my empty glass a while and said it was my round."
]},
{ id:"bazaar", cat:"desire", place:"bazaar", title:"The Bazaar", body:[
"Over the Marketplace hangs a tent so large that the sun, coming through it, makes a second and redder sky. It has been patched and re-dyed so often that its first color is a matter for argument. Under it each face looks flushed and each blade looks warm, and that is no accident. Red light flatters merchandise. It also hides blood, and how pale a buyer goes when he counts out his coins.",
"For one gold piece you can buy your own face in soapstone, carved by a blind half-orc who learns it with her fingers. For a few silver, a strip of cloth soaked at last Sar's executions, priced by the fame of the man who bled on it. For twenty gold, a map of the floor of the Thunder Sea; for two hundred, a map of Kythri, where nothing holds still long enough to be mapped. A gnome named Hanruli sells tapestries she dreamed, and in one of them you will find yourself impaled on the iron railing of a black tower beside a stormy sea. She is as frightened of the likeness as you are. She'll sell it to you anyway.",
"The traders here would sell their own mothers, the saying goes, and they bid against each other for the privilege. Very little under the red cloth is fake and nothing is cheap. Each stall prices its goods by how badly you want them, and the sellers have had generations to learn to read wanting. The ones who misread it don't last a season. High over the walkways an acrobat named Nat Gann balances on a pole for coppers, and sells what he sees from up there to anyone who pays better."
]},
{ id:"shadows", cat:"desire", place:"shadows", title:"Shadows", body:[
"The best brothel in Xen'drik has no sign and no address, and the price of finding it starts at somebody else's door. You buy a word from a man who knows a man. The man asks you what shadows seek. You say illumination, because you paid to know that, and he looks you over the way a Kundarak clerk looks over a letter of credit. If you pass, you get directions and a second word, and the second word is already going stale.",
"Inside, the elves of House Thuranni pour the best spirits on the continent in rooms where the lamps are kept low on purpose. You tell them what you want. It comes up the stairs. Later, in the dark, drunk on the spirits and on yourself, you tell them other things: the cargo that sails on Wir, the name of the clerk who takes your bribes, which of your partners you mean to cut out. Most clients don't remember a word of it in the morning. The few who do trust the house to keep quiet, and it does. It keeps everything. Harbor Lord Jonas Wylkes spends more nights here than at his desk, and the Harbor runs a little worse each season, and someone in Locksmith Square knows exactly how much worse.",
"The elves keep no enclave in Stormreach and take no side among the lords. When a man with powerful enemies goes missing, people say the word <em>Shadows</em> once, and then they talk about the weather."
]},
{ id:"pirates-moon", cat:"desire", place:"harbor-docks", title:"Pirate's Moon", body:[
"On the twentieth of Dravago the whole city walks down to the water carrying things. Bread, rings, coins, a roast fowl, ribbons, a letter that was never sent. At the ends of the piers they throw it all into the harbor, and the sahuagin watching from the swells let it sink. The Bilge Rats work the crowd on the way down, so that by the time a family reaches the edge the offering is sometimes all it has left.",
"The offerings are for the Devourer. In the city's first hard years the pirate lord Jaldrin Arekarn and his lover, the dread captain Kilen Razorheart, broke an invading armada in this harbor, and while they embraced over the victory a traitor's arrow took them both. They went over the side together and were never found. The histories do not name the archer. In Stormreach, when a killer goes unnamed, it usually means somebody paid for the silence as well as the arrow. The city feeds the god each spring so that he will leave the lovers alone.",
"It is also the festival of love. The young court each other on the piers while the gifts go down around them, and some cut their palms and press them together, the strongest promise Stormreach knows. Late in the evening I watched a girl throw in a sealed letter, wait until it darkened and turned under, then take her young man by the hand and pull him quickly away from the edge, before the sea could ask for a reply."
]},
{ id:"black-wrack", cat:"signs", place:"black-wrack", title:"The Black Wrack", body:[
"The north wall of the Black Wrack, which calls itself the biggest tavern in Stormreach, cannot be seen. Notices cover it, nailed over notices, and the landlord, a big good-natured man named Handlon whom everyone calls Brick, sorts them by kind, work with work and the missing with the missing. His rule is that all are welcome and any quarrel goes outside. His mastiff, Sascha, walks a slow circuit of the floor, and no drinker, however drunk, involves her in anything. Half the jobs that leave this city for the jungle start on that wall, and so do most of the funerals. Here it is as it stood one evening.",
"<em>Guide wanted. Must speak Giant. No pay in advance.</em> <em>Lost, one brother, last seen at the Tents. Red beard. Owes me money.</em> <em>Strong backs for a short dig. No questions.</em> <em>Witness wanted, Harbor, night of the ninth. Discretion paid for. Silence paid better.</em> <em>Will buy any map, any condition. Will pay ten times as much for a true one.</em> <em>Wanted, anyone who has come back from the Cinder Sisters. Only to talk.</em> <em>Found, one hand, with ring. Ask at the bar.</em>",
"The ceiling is high enough for the minotaur Kolos to stand upright, and most nights he stands in front of the wall with a cup and reads it top to bottom. He has never answered a notice. When Brick asked him why he bothered, he said he was checking whether any of them were about him."
]},
{ id:"circle-of-visions", cat:"signs", place:"circle-of-visions", title:"The Circle of Visions", body:[
"Twelve rings of standing stones are scattered through the city, and the people who raised them are forty thousand years dead. About once a month, on a night no almanac predicts, a picture rises in the middle of one ring and hangs there until dawn. Sometimes it is a garden. Sometimes it is a giant whose name has been lost, standing as if about to speak. Sometimes it is something that frightens the children, and the priests call it a warning, and the fortune-tellers sell the warning back to the people who saw it.",
"Lately an old hill giant from the Tents walks from ring to ring at dusk with a staff and a basket of herbs, sits with his back against the stones, and waits. His name is Gaulronak. He speaks for the Tents to the Storm Lords, which makes him the most patient diplomat in Stormreach, and he has lived beside this city for nearly two hundred years. He sits at the stones the way a Sentinel Marshal sits outside a door, sure that whatever is inside will have to come out sometime.",
"I sat with him one night in the ring in the Marketplace. Nothing came. Toward morning I asked what the pictures were for. The carvers knew they would forget, he said, so they made the stones remember for them. But no one told the stones which memories mattered, so they show all of them at the same rate, the gardens and the warnings alike, to whoever happens to be sitting there. Then he picked up his basket and walked on to the next ring. By then it was plain that he was not waiting for just any picture. He had one in mind, and he would not tell me which."
]},
{ id:"tattered-alice", cat:"signs", place:"fishers-folly", title:"Tattered Alice", body:[
"In Fisher's Folly, among the nets and the leaning huts, a woman called Tattered Alice tears at her clothes with her nails and shouts what is coming. Dark gods will wake under the harbor. The sea devils will rise in a plague. The Storm Lords will poison the water. She has shouted for years, and not one of her prophecies has come true. Anywhere else that would make her a madwoman. The fishers of the Folly have reached the opposite verdict, which is that each disaster, once Alice has said it aloud, is used up and can no longer happen. So before a hard voyage they bring her fish heads and a coin, sit on an upturned hull at her feet, and ask her to name the worst. She names it. They thank her. They go out past the Talon in weather that would keep a Lyrandar captain in port, and most of them come back, and the Folly takes this as proof of nothing except what it knew already. The Storm Lords, for their part, have never once poisoned the water, and the fishers will tell you exactly whom to thank. The only thing they fear is the morning Alice wakes up hoarse."
]},
{ id:"rigging", cat:"rigging", place:"cross", title:"The Rigging", body:[
"To cross Stormreach you will need your hands. The city was built for people four times your height, and the people who live in it now have had to rig it like a ship before they could sail it. Where a step comes up to your chest, someone has cut a smaller step into it. Where a wall has no door, someone has hung a knotted rope. Over Cross the rope bridges run so thick from ruin to ruin that the sky comes down in strips, with laundry hanging in them, and a man who knows the bridges can be three streets away before the man chasing him has found the stairs.",
"Learn the colors. New hemp is gold and will hold you. Grey hemp has seen a few wet seasons and will probably hold you. Black hemp is older than anyone's grandmother, and only those born on it walk on it; they'll pass you at a run while you are still deciding. Learn also that some houses hang from the undersides of floating stones, that their owners draw up the ladders at night and sleep swaying, and that if you call up to them after dark, the ladder stays up.",
"There are people in Cross who haven't set foot on the giants' pavement in years. The Guard walks the pavement. Ask them the way to the Marketplace and they will point up."
]},
{ id:"leaky-dinghy", cat:"rigging", place:"fishers-folly", title:"The Leaky Dinghy", body:[
"Fisher's Folly is a heap of boats, poles, huts and nets lashed together at the harbor's edge so that each holds up the others, and in the middle of the heap stands a tavern called the Leaky Dinghy. It rots. Rats run in its rafters. The floor leans east in the morning and west by night. Sahuagin come in out of the rain to hire on as guides across the Thunder Sea. Next door, in a shack, a halfling called Capshaw the Crier prints leaflets against the Storm Lords and the dragonmarked houses, and calls the Dinghy the only honest building on the waterfront, because it has never pretended to be sound.",
"By any reckoning it should have gone down years ago. Its regulars won't allow it. They patch it after each storm with whatever the storm brought in, and during the worst storms, when shingles are coming off the Harbormaster's house up the hill, they crowd inside and hold a party. They drink to the beams by name. They cheer each gust that fails. When the wind finally drops they go out along the swaying planks to count what is missing. The Harbormaster's house has lost its shingles again. The Dinghy has lost a shutter. They nail the shutter back with the Harbormaster's shingles."
]},
{ id:"rubble-warren", cat:"rigging", place:"rubble-warren", title:"The Rubble Warren", body:[
"Nobody in Stormreach quarries stone. The giants left more than any city could use, and the people of the present break it up and carry it off. On the northern edge of Saltire there is a field where the blocks are broken, and it is called the Rubble Warren. Bees have hived in its cracks, and their honey goes into the little cakes at Beehive Pastries in the Sloths, where the baker, Sulman, sells the cakes over the counter and what he overhears under it.",
"The children of the ward have dug tunnels through the broken stone and given them names. A twelve-year-old called Cadroc leads a band of them who mean to be explorers. They practice with wooden swords and keep a chalk map of the Warren on a flat slab at its center, and Cadroc will tell you very seriously which tunnels are dangerous. He has heard the adults complain about rats underneath, and he means to deal with them himself. Each month the stone-breakers cart off a few more blocks for some wall in Cross, and a tunnel goes with them, and the boys rub it off the slab and draw in the new ones.",
"I crawled through one tunnel at Cadroc's invitation. Its walls had been the floor of a giant's house, and before that a quarry the giants cut, and next year they will be the wall of a boarding house in Cross, and after that, when the boarding house falls, rubble again, for children not yet born to dig through and name."
]},
{ id:"harbor", cat:"trade", place:"harbor", title:"The Harbor", body:[
"Everything in Stormreach runs downhill into the harbor. Rain goes down, and sewage, and rumor, and the blood from the Marketplace on Sar, and now and then a man who owed the Bilge Rats money, and the outflow of the Waterworks, which the gulls don't seem to mind. The water smells like a ledger with all of it entered. On the piers a hill giant and two orcs of House Tharashk load dragonshards onto a Lyrandar ship. A goblin haggles with two elves of Aerenal over a crate wrapped in leaves. A dwarf of the Sovereign Host and a human in the silver of the Flame argue doctrine with a sahuagin priest who wears seaweed and the bones of something large. The hobgoblins on the slave ships don't look up. The guard at the end of the pier doesn't look at anyone, which is what he is paid for.",
"Coin from every mint in the world crosses these planks, and none of it is asked where it's been. In a pirate town, they say, all tender is legal. The scales at the duty-house are honest. The man reading them isn't. Berrigan Enge is a half-elf, a distant cousin of the Harbor Lord, and he has been skimming so long and so steadily that the merchants price him in, like weather. I watched him take a duty payment from a woman with a garrote coiled at her belt, write her a receipt, and wish her a profitable evening. A loaf in Cross costs a copper more because of him. Everyone in Cross knows the exact figure, and they say it the way other cities say a saint's name."
]},
{ id:"von-ruthveks", cat:"trade", place:"von-ruthveks", title:"Von Ruthvek's Splendors of the South", body:[
"A clay tablet the size of a door comes into Stormreach on the shoulders of four porters and is carried up to the shop of Valexa Von Ruthvek, the best-known antiquities dealer in the city, a gnome who keeps her stock in labeled cases like a museum and can date a broken lamp by the wear on its handle. She dates the tablet to the Age of Giants. Two of the expedition that found it did not come back, and she adds a line for each to the card that will travel with it, in small upright letters.",
"In a cellar across the city a second tablet the size of a door is drying on trestles. Its clay was dug from the riverbank on Wir. A journeyman of the Hollow Shards has aged it with cold tea and a hot iron, and a senior forger is cutting the last line of an inscription in a Giant unspoken for forty thousand years, and that he speaks rather well. It will be more beautiful than the first. Several of its elder brothers stand in warded cases at Morgrave University under labels that call them landmark discoveries, and on holidays the forgers take their apprentices to look.",
"Von Ruthvek's expeditions are paid for now by patrons she has never met, through intermediaries, on one condition, that each go deeper into the wilds than the last. The latest is after something called the Vessel of the Infinite Sea. Her patrons do not want it brought back to her; they have hired other people to take it off her people on the road home, before she can read in it what they want it for. She does not know this. She knows only that each expedition comes home with less, and with fewer of the people who went, and that the cards she writes grow longer every season."
]},
{ id:"tents-of-rusheme", cat:"trade", place:"tents-of-rusheme", title:"The Tents of Rushemé", body:[
"Across the river from Southwatch the giants keep their market. The tents are as tall as houses, and the people of the city walk among them like children among furniture. The giants are nomads of several tribes, mostly hill and jungle giants, and they come to this border to trade, to keep their rites, and to be near the city that belonged to their ancestors. They are careful with the people who live in their ancestors' houses. They are not friendly. The Guard keeps a close watch on them.",
"On certain nights, at sunset, they sing. In Southwatch people stop what they are doing. The sound arrives through the soles of the feet before the ears have it. A Karrn woman on her doorstep told me she had lived beside the Tents for eleven years and had never asked what the songs were about, and did not mean to. She went in before the end. Her neighbors stayed out until the last note.",
"Afterward the giants make burnt offerings. They are for the titans, the first giants, who were cruel, and who are said to be waiting somewhere past death for a way back, and the offerings are meant to keep them content where they are. In the morning there is ash on the river and a fat smell of roasting over Southwatch, and the Guard posts an extra man at the ford and gives him no orders."
]},
{ id:"silverwall", cat:"eyes", place:"locksmith-square", title:"Locksmith Square", body:[
"The walls around Locksmith Square rise sixty feet and shine like silver on the side that faces the square, and only on that side; from the streets outside they are grey stone. Inside, you are seen twice. Your lantern goes away from you along the walls in both directions. The acrobat above the crowd tumbles above herself. When the Red Ring lets out after a bloody match, the crowd leaving the arena is joined by a second crowd, blurred and bright, leaving with it. The reflections are never clear enough to show you your own face. They are clear enough to show you that someone is watching, that the someone has turned your way, and that you have turned to watch them back. At night the silver spreads the lamplight thin and doubles each shadow, and a square with twice the shadows has twice the places to stand in one. Somewhere in the square the elves of Shadows are listening, and somewhere above it the gnome lady Kirris Sel Shadra, who runs more of the city's crime than the Guard ever catches, is said to be listening to them. Stand still long enough in the middle of the square and you will notice that you have begun to walk the way the people who live there walk, with your head a little turned, like someone listening for a step at your shoulder."
]},
{ id:"eldreds-pool", cat:"eyes", place:"eldreds-pool", title:"Eldred's Pool", body:[
"At the center of Locksmith Square there is a basin of dark green stone, twenty-four feet across, from which the ward draws its water. Whatever is poured into it comes out clean. Rain, bilge, wine and poison all come out the same, which in this ward is a mercy and in some households a disappointment. In the middle two six-limbed figures stand back to back, each balanced on one leg, one pair of hands pressed flat together, the other pair raised to hold up something that is not there. Their stone heads are bowed.",
"The thri-kreen made it. Tens of thousands of them lived here once, cutting tunnels into the giants' foundations, and then, as far as anyone can tell, they were all gone at the same moment. In the city's first years a scholar named Eldred grew obsessed with the basin. He paid ruffians to chip at it and bring him samples of its water. At last he went to the Storm Lords and demanded that they destroy it, because, he said, it endangered everyone. They asked for proof. He had none he would say aloud, and they sent him away. By the next day he was gone. No one was ever charged, which in Stormreach is not the same as no one being guilty.",
"The few who know such things say the pool sends home anything that does not belong to this world, if the thing sees its own reflection in the water. The Riedran consul warns the Inspired among his countrymen to look away when they pass. I filled a cup there each morning while I lodged in the square, and looked down each time, and each time the water showed me the same tired traveler under the two bowed heads, and left him where he was."
]},
{ id:"red-ring", cat:"eyes", place:"red-ring", title:"The Red Ring", body:[
"The magistrates of Stormreach have two sentences for serious crimes. One is death, carried out on Sar in the square at the foot of the palace steps, with families bringing their lunch. The other is the Red Ring, where a convict may fight his way out; win a title bout and the city paroles you. Which sentence you get is up to Storm Lord Kirris Sel Shadra, who oversees the courts, and the stablemasters pay her a fee for each strong back she sends them. The stands fill nightly. Everyone bets, down to the occasional Storm Lord, and some of the money is dirty when it goes on the table and clean when it comes off. Some of the fighters are the bloodbound, sworn to the arena for life, and the stablemasters are known to lend them to the Guard when someone needs to die quietly.",
"I watched from the high seats as a Valenar elf ducked a giant's club to the roar of the crowd. His people tell of a hero, Dyrael Morain, who led the largest elf army ever raised against this city in the age of the giants, and died with all of it. Valenar warriors still search the fields south of Stormreach for his bones and his blade. This one had crossed the sea to stand where his ancestor fell, and he had found the place. A seat cost a copper. The club came round again."
]},
{ id:"recruiters", cat:"names", place:"recruiters", title:"The Stormreach Recruiters", body:[
"Near the palace there is an office with a ledger, and the ledger sells the one thing Stormreach can't steal from you, which is a clean start. Sign on with the Stormreach Guard, last out three months of training, and whatever you did before is wiped off the books. The Storm Lords have shipped fugitives home to the Five Nations before. They have never shipped home a guard. The pay is three silver a day. The bribes are extra, and the recruiters will tell you so if you ask politely. Most recruits take a new name to go with the new start, and the clerk writes it in the roll beside the old one and inks the old one out.",
"Some names from this year's roll, as the clerks recall them. Steady. Ransom. Clean. Ash, and then a second Ash, entered as Ash Again. Merit. Tomorrow. Mercy, which three women asked for in the same month. One man couldn't think of anything and wrote down his own name, and was told it wasn't allowed.",
"The officers who take the oaths share one broad, bearded face, because most of them are Yorrick Amanatu's children, and the old dwarf in the mithral shirt was reaving on the Thunder Sea before any of them were born. His Guard holds deserters and war criminals, a fallen paladin or two, and a handful who believe in the law and are made miserable by the rest. On patrol they meet their old selves in the taverns and aren't obliged to say hello. I asked one, a kind and very tired woman, what she'd been called before. She said she didn't remember. Then she said she remembered exactly, and that was why she'd come, and her name now was Mercy, and would I please move along."
]},
{ id:"emperor", cat:"names", place:"emperor", title:"The Emperor", body:[
"Every ship that makes Stormreach sees it before it sees the city, and each crew aboard has its own name for it.",
"The scholars of Morgrave call it Cul'sir, after the titan who made this coast the heart of his empire, and write in their margins that it was set there against the demons of the past. Lyrandar pilots call it the Lamp and line their bowsprits on the column of light that rises out of its joined palms; the house has never paid it a copper for the service. The sahuagin have a word for it that the viceroy will translate only as <em>the dry one</em>. The wizards who have spent careers on its aura file reports that call it, once the expenses are totaled, very strong. The children of the Harbor call it the Emperor and don't ask what it ruled. Their mothers tell them it watches over them, and point.",
"Its shoulders are white with the droppings of a hundred thousand seasons of gulls. The goat path up the cliff gives out well below its feet, at a ledge where the goats themselves turn back, and above the ledge the grass grows to the knee, uncut and unflattened. It is the only grass in Stormreach no one has walked on, and the only ground in the city without a price on it."
]},
{ id:"god-man", cat:"names", place:"temple-row", title:"Temple Row", body:[
"Temple Row turns over its gods each week, like a Night Tide table turning over its stock. Priests of the Sovereign Host bark beside preachers of the Dark Six; a stall selling silver flames does business next to one selling clay figures of things that live below the world. Under the colored lanterns everyone wants you to pray, and each prayer has a bowl set out in front of it.",
"Among them, sleeping in whichever shrine is empty that night, is a man who woke from a fever knowing the names of forgotten gods. He carries them on his skin as tattoos and in his pockets as dolls, and if you stop he'll introduce you. Abassa, goddess of pear cider. Zelthanon, god of cocked dice, who decides the throws that land against the wall; the dicers of the Harbor slip him a copper before a big game, which is more than most gods on the Row take in. Humoona, goddess of lemon zest, without whom your sweet cakes would be nothing. The god of the second stocking. The goddess of the moment before a sneeze. The small god who keeps the food warm under a cloth. He adds a new one every few weeks, and the dolls keep getting smaller, because his pockets are full.",
"<em>Praise not the great gods,</em> he says. <em>They don't need your prayers. Praise the little ones, whose names you never knew.</em> He sets out no bowl. The priests on either side have stopped trying to move him along, since he draws a crowd and the crowd has to walk past their bowls to reach him. In the evenings a few people stop in front of him, buy nothing, and go home more careful with the lemons."
]},
{ id:"delera-watch", cat:"dead", place:"delera-watch", title:"Delera's Watch", body:[
"In Respite the dead outnumber the living, and they are stacked three floors deep. On top are the tombs of Delera's Watch, where Storm Lords, pirate captains and rich explorers lie with their gold and their maps behind traps, wards and guardians that never sleep, because a pirate meant to keep what he had taken even after he was finished with it. Around those tombs the poor have buried their own for generations, in shallow graves, or out in the open for the birds where their customs ask it, so that the necropolis of the rich stands in a moat of bones. Under all of it are the barrows of the giants, and under the barrows, the people of Respite say, pits of dead who have lain undisturbed for forty thousand years.",
"The floors don't stay apart. The dead rise here more often than they should, and the newer tombs are warded to keep their tenants in, not thieves out. House Jorasco does a steady trade raising the wealthy of Respite at prices that would buy a ship. When the house tried it on Delera Omaren herself, a founder of the city, the woman who sat up on the slab cried out in the language of the giants and killed dozens with lightning before she could be put back down. Since then Jorasco's healers cast an augury before they raise anyone in Respite, and the first thing they ask is which floor the answer is coming from."
]},
{ id:"crypt-of-the-guard", cat:"dead", place:"crypt-of-the-guard", title:"The Crypt of the Guard", body:[
"The Crypt of the Guard keeps the tide's hours. It was dug a century ago for the Guard's fallen, a few yards too close to the water, and twice a day the sea fills its vaults to the lintels and the dead get their privacy. Twice a day it drains out through the cracks, and for about an hour you can walk the aisles ankle-deep among the crabs.",
"The city meant it as an honor. It came out as an accusation. The masons laid the dead in the order they died, and the Guard has always been made of men starting over, so the traitors went in beside the heroes, slab against slab, under the names they took when they signed. No record says which were which. The men who could have said are in the walls with the rest.",
"The guards still breathing have drawn their own conclusions. They put by what they can from three silver a day, and from what the bribes bring in, to be buried anywhere but here. The Emerald Claw has come more than once at low water and left with soldiers, the only time in a century the Crypt's dead have been called back to duty. Now it takes paupers. When the tide turns a keeper walks ahead of the water with a lantern, checking the seals, and the paupers' slabs are the only ones he bothers to read."
]},
{ id:"old-catacombs", cat:"dead", place:"old-catacombs", title:"The Old Catacombs", body:[
"Near the wall between the Marketplace and Dannel's Pride, the top of a giant's tower juts out of the street at an angle, with the rest of it buried. The Silver Flame used it as a sanctuary and, when the sanctuary filled up, as a prison. Five years ago the dead came up through it. Most were put down. The rest were walled into the lower floors behind wards and new masonry, the upper floors were abandoned, and the Flame stopped mentioning the place in its sermons.",
"Beside the sealed door a dwarf smeared with ash beats a drum day and night and recites the names of the dead. He is Yannick Drumdoom, and he serves the Keeper. He takes tokens from tombs, a ring, a buckle, a curl of hair folded in paper, so that their owners will stay close to the world, and the families of those owners would very much like a word with him. People cross the street. I stood and listened for most of an hour, and he didn't repeat a name. When he stopped to drink I asked where he found so many. He pointed down with the drumstick. Then he started again, and the beat followed me across the Marketplace, slower than my heart, and that night in my rented room I found I was counting it."
]},
{ id:"floating-ruins", cat:"sky", place:"floating-ruins", title:"The Floating Ruins", body:[
"Once there were fortresses in the air over this city, and mansions, and towers that floated. Time and war broke them, and the pieces still hang over the streets. Some never move. Others drift on slow circuits that the old people know the way fishers know tides, and in Respite there are households that keep time by them. The broken balcony crosses the sun at the hour for bread. The cistern-stone shades the Garden of Respite at noon, and is blessed for it in summer and cursed for it in winter. The long stone the children call the Boot comes round every nineteen days and blacks out the moon over Stormhaven for a night, and on those nights the burglars of the city work the manors, and the rich lock their doors and sit up with a crossbow across their knees.",
"Up there, ownership works the way it does on the ground. A few wealthy families have patched the larger fragments and live on them, out of reach of their creditors. The poor hang wooden houses from the undersides of the smaller ones, climb up by knotted rope, and pull the rope up after them at night. The prettiest stones have gathered seabird droppings for centuries until a seed took, and then another, and now islands of flowers ride over Respite with their roots trailing in the wind. There are no deeds to those. When the wind turns, petals come down on the funerals in Delera's Watch, and the mourners look up and can't decide whom to thank."
]},
{ id:"falconers-spire", cat:"sky", place:"falconers-spire", title:"Falconer's Spire", body:[
"The weather of one month, as the fishers of the Folly chalked it on a beam in the Leaky Dinghy.",
"<em>Sul. Rain from the south. Mol. Rain. Zol. Hail at noon, two boats stove in at their moorings, the Lyrandar ships beside them untouched. Wir. Calm. Zor. Calm, and a squall out of a clear sky off the Talon, one boat lost with three. Far. Fog. Sar. Fog. Second week. Fair wind for three days, the week the Marketplace merchants signed their new contracts with Lyrandar. Lightning twice on one roof in Cross, the roof of a man who had not signed. Rain. Rain.</em>",
"Beside some of the days a fisher has chalked a mark that means <em>honest</em>. Beside most there is nothing.",
"Falconer's Spire rises out of the Marketplace taller than the palace, inlaid with blue and hung with moored airships. In its highest room an old half-elf named Calynden d'Lyrandar, whom his own house calls the Kraken because he is never seen until he strikes, reads the harbor reports, then closes his eyes over a chart of the Thunder Sea and feels for the paths of the storms. He came here as a seneschal his matriarch wanted out of her sight. Since he arrived the old Harbor Lord has died of causes no healer would name, the young one has been buried in gifts and galas, and Lyrandar has won the eastern peninsula for a shipyard. His house once sold fair weather in Khorvaire, to farmers and to generals. In Stormreach it has never been proved to sell anything of the kind. On the beam in the Dinghy the second month is already begun, in a different hand."
]},
{ id:"lighthouse", cat:"sky", place:"lighthouse", title:"The Lighthouse", body:[
"The lighthouse has no stairs. It was raised on its rock in the harbor nearly as long ago as the Emperor, and it is solid stone the whole way up, and at the top a crystal no hand has touched turns by itself, throwing its beam out over the sea, then back across the roofs of the city, then out again. The smugglers time their boats by it.",
"Only the ground floor and the cellars are hollow, and Eldamir Fallowcrest has filled them with books. You may read one at a time. He stands at your shoulder while you do it, close enough that you hear him breathe, and when you turn a leaf he watches the leaf and not you. At the first sign of trouble he locks the library and sets its traps, and he alone decides what the first sign was. Some of his visitors, the harbor says, never did find out.",
"I read a sailing almanac there one afternoon. At dusk the crystal began to turn, and its beam came in through the one high window and crossed my page, and went, and came again. Each time it crossed, Eldamir's lips moved. When I handed the book back I saw on its flyleaf a column of small strokes in his hand, tied in bundles of five, running down the page and onto the next, and the last stroke was still wet."
]},
{ id:"scuppers", cat:"storms", place:"scuppers", title:"The Scuppers", body:[
"The giants built drains big enough to empty a storm in an hour. Since then the drains have been choked with rubbish or sealed from below by whatever lives in them, and when the rain comes the water has nowhere to go but the streets.",
"So Stormreach builds on stilts, and when a street floods, its people cut a channel to send the water into the next street. The next street cuts one of its own. Each household knows exactly whose door its water goes to and whose water comes to its door, and a good share of the city's feuds are older than anyone's memory of the channel that started them; there are knifings in Cross that trace back to a trench dug before the knifer's grandfather was born. By the time a storm is over the water has found its way, house by house and grudge by grudge, into a few broad avenues that pour into the harbor. These are the scuppers, after the holes in a ship's deck. Newcomers drown in them each year, not knowing to keep clear, and wash up a day later with their pockets already emptied. Nobody designed the system and everybody resents it. The engineers the Storm Lords once sent to replace it went home and reported that it works, which the city did not want to hear."
]},
{ id:"burning-titan", cat:"storms", place:"titan-cliffs", title:"The Burning Titan", body:[
"For six days at the end of winter the city builds a giant on the cliffs east of the Lyrandar shipyard, out of wicker, timber and oil. Most of Forgelight gets the week off to help. House Phiarlan supplies the music around the clock, at rates it doesn't publish. The builders go through dozens of casks of gin, and the titan rises over the sea with its arms lifted.",
"At sundown on the first of Therendor they set it alight. The crowd wears dragon masks and dances all night, because dragons burned the giants' empire to the ground, and this is the festival of that burning. Across the river the giants of Rushemé watch the glow. They find it distasteful, and they take the insult the way a Kundarak banker takes a late payment, without comment and without forgetting.",
"I danced too, behind a paper dragon's face, until the titan's arms fell in and threw sparks out over the water. Then I turned around. Up the coast the Emperor stood on his cliff, lit orange from below and white from his own hands, with his back to all of us, facing the dark sea. A child beside me had turned as well. She looked from the burning giant to the stone one and back again, took off her mask, and then put it on again."
]},
{ id:"shacklebreak", cat:"storms", place:"marketplace", title:"Shacklebreak", body:[
"On the first of Aryth the law takes the day off. No crime short of murder or arson is punished. Most of the Guard stays home. The Storm Lords grant amnesty to a crowd of prisoners, the cells under the Red Ring swing open, and masks come out of chests all over the city. On Shacklebreak you may smash a shopfront and walk away. You may fight a stranger, or kiss one, or rob one, or all three in that order. You may sing the songs the Swords of Karrn would take a finger for, throw a chair on the bonfire in the middle of the Marketplace, climb the palace railings and shout up at the windows. You may not burn a house or kill a man, though each year somebody forgets which day it is, and each year a few debts get settled behind masks, at a discount no other day offers. The city was a pirate haven before it was a city, and it keeps this one day for the memory. Some call the day a fever, a small dose of Du'rashka Tul, the old madness that turned the giants on one another and ended their nation, and they say that one year the dose will be too large. Others call it the only honest day in the calendar. In the morning the shop boards go back up and the Guard comes back to collect its bribes, and for a week afterward people in the street look at one another's faces longer than usual, trying to fit them to masks."
]},
{ id:"grindstone", cat:"oaths", place:"grindstone", title:"Grindstone", body:[
"Come through the gate into Grindstone and the air changes. Doors close. Children stop their game to watch you. Only the street vendors meet your eye. People from other districts walk fast here, and the people of Grindstone are proud of that.",
"This is the Karrnathi ward, and it is the only place in Stormreach where theft, brawling and indecency are punished. The Swords of Karrn see to it, under an exiled warlord named Drago Thul who crossed the sea still hunting for justice and has settled for order. Steal here and they will dock a finger, so that each time you look at the stump you'll remember who runs this street. The Guard doesn't come in without orders, and seldom with them. In the middle of the ward a temple called Rosewood stands inside the skeleton of a giants' ruin, grown over with a crimson ivy as tough as armor, and a quiet herbalist named Berrinessa keeps the rites of the Blood of Vol there in the open, as her people could not at home. Some thugs once tried to rob her in the street, and half the ward stoned them to death. You will be safer in Grindstone than anywhere in the Harbor. You will not feel safer. When you leave by the far gate you'll catch yourself counting your fingers, and the people of Summerfield on the other side will smile at you, because they have seen everyone do it."
]},
{ id:"whitewash", cat:"oaths", place:"whitewash", title:"Whitewash and Embers", body:[
"Each morning the magewrights of Oldgate speak a word over the streets and the dust gets up and leaves. The rubble has been carted off, the houses painted, the moss scraped from the giants' stones, and travelers who crossed an ocean to see a ruin complain that the district looks newer than the towns they left. The Storm Lords call it a model district. At its western end the great inland gate of the giants still stands, unwashed, because no magewright can reach it.",
"Whitewash is the Aundairian half. It burns more everbright lanterns than any other ward in Stormreach, and in its square a fountain cleans whatever is dipped in it, so the collars of Whitewash are the whitest in the city and its people make sure you notice. Embers is the Thrane half. It keeps to its houses and its prayers and to Commander Valen Vanatar, whose Knights begin each meal with a toast to Queen Diani and the day she takes back her throne, and who advise Karrns, and anyone who prays to the Six, to walk around.",
"Aundair and Thrane made peace at Thronehold two years ago, on the other side of the sea. The treaty was read aloud on both sides of the street that divides the wards, and both sides went home and oiled their weapons. The Ninth Wands still walk one pavement with war-wands at their belts. The Knights still walk the other. In the morning the street between them is cleaned twice, once by a magewright from Whitewash and once by a magewright from Embers, and each half of Oldgate stands at its windows to see that the other half's magewright does it properly."
]},
{ id:"undercity", cat:"hidden", place:"waterworks", title:"The Waterworks", body:[
"Where the Koronoo meets the harbor, the city's pipes empty into the sea, and behind the pipes and the steam vents stand iron doors, sealed. Here, in order of depth, is what lies behind them.",
"First, the kobolds, who crawled up out of the deep earth some time in the last century. The Guard pays two coppers a hide and doesn't ask whose hide it was.",
"Then the sewers of the giants, vaulted like temples and older the farther you go. Then the tunnels of the thri-kreen, cut in curves no human hand would choose and lined with monuments to something they never explained. Then the flooded halls where the sahuagin once built a town in the giants' drains, which the sahuagin of today refuse to speak of. Then the streets of the giants' own city, buried whole and less worn than anything on the surface, with dust on the tables and the chairs pushed back.",
"Under that, the old records say, the wizards of Cul'sir were working on a source of power so great that they never wrote down plainly what it was. Whether they built it or found it is not recorded. Several expeditions have been paid to find out. The payments are recorded.",
"A thri-kreen guide took me through the second door and some way past the third. He had chosen me; that is how it is done with the thri-kreen. He explained nothing and charged nothing, which worried me more than a price would have. After an hour we came up again into the noise and stink of the harbor, where the fishwives were shouting and a ship was unloading oranges, and everyone on the quay was standing with their full weight on the stones, trusting them to go all the way down."
]},
{ id:"kol-korran", cat:"hidden", place:"kol-korran", title:"The Tower of Kol Korran", body:[
"A great trench marks the place where the giants stopped building, at the eastern edge of the city, and everyone after them stopped there too. The ground beyond it is flat and the harbor beside it deep. Yorrick Amanatu brokered its sale to House Kundarak, every yard from the harbor to the river mouth, and has done well out of the dwarves since. They crossed the trench with two stone bridges, and at the far end of them even the Stormreach Guard can be turned away. The Storm Lords put their seals to a paper saying so.",
"From the roofs of Silverwall you can see the Tower of Kol Korran, which is a ring. A wall several feet thick runs round it in a perfect circle, and inside the circle there is sky. A single gatehouse, warded on every stone, lets into the courtyard. What stands in the courtyard can be told only by its shadow, which at evening falls across the wall and out onto the street, a great deal taller than the wall.",
"Three things are known about the tower. The faithful of Kol Korran, god of wealth, came to pray at it in its first months, and the guards at the gate told them it was not a temple. The guards said it was a place where matters of house security were tested. Burglars went to test it in their turn, and none of them has been seen since. A fourth thing is known in the taverns of Locksmith Square: someone in the city, who never gives a name, keeps paying good money to hire more.",
"The lamps on the two bridges burn all night. In the evening the dwarves walk out across them on business in the city, each with one guard, or two, or three, and at the far end each of them looks back once at the ring of wall, the way a man pats his coat to feel that the purse is still there."
]},
{ id:"underharbor", cat:"hidden", place:"shargons-talon", title:"Shargon's Talon", body:[
"The harbor has a floor of streets. Under the moorings, behind curtains of weed and crusts of mussel shell, there are arches and doorways and stairs going down into darker water. The sahuagin live there. They walk the docks in peace when they come ashore, hire on as guides for the crossing, and dine with the Lyrandar viceroy, who talks with their priests in their own tongue about matters he doesn't report to his house. Lyrandar ships cross the Thunder Sea unmolested. Other ships hire guides, and pay what the guides ask.",
"In the middle of the harbor a slender tower of stone rises out of the water, more like a stalagmite than a building, with flames burning in its narrow windows. It is the temple of the Devourer, and two priests keep it, an elf and a sahuagin. The faithful swim to it. Rowing is permitted, and despised. Lately a member of the Aurum has started making the swim in the early mornings, and the harbor is trying to work out what he is buying.",
"I swam out on a still morning. Halfway there I put my face in the water and saw the second city under the first, its streets and doorways and the shapes moving in them. The sahuagin teach that the Devourer raised the land out of the sea in a battle at the beginning of the world and will one day draw it all back under. Nobody down there seemed to be in a hurry. I reached the Talon with my lungs burning and held on to its stone, and an acolyte with scales leaned out of a window and looked me over with courtesy, the kind you show a cousin who has done well enough, considering."
]}
];

// Vignettes, part B (factions, Chapters Two to Four)
const CITIES_B = [
{ id:"molous", cat:"memory", place:"molous", title:"Molou's Distillery", body:[
"The smell gets to you before the building does, hot berries and smoke with something sharp underneath, and on a warm wind it lies over Cross and the Marketplace for hours. Molou's Distillery makes kuryeva, the jungle-berry gin they drink in every port of southern Khorvaire. When the pirates of the Thunder Sea cut their deal with Galifar, the other captains brought ships and swords to the table. Molou Lassite, smuggler and master brewer, brought casks, and plenty of people will tell you he bought the peace with them. A century later word came that King Jarot was dead and Galifar was tearing itself to pieces, and the Lassite of the day poured a free shot for anyone who would drink to the kingdom's fall. The line ran down to the docks.",
"These days the distillery also pours sooka, kuryeva with a little dreamlily cut into it, which goes down gentler and lets go of you harder. The Lassites are the only Storm Lords their people love, and they have worked out what love costs. Lord Varen cannot always buy justice for a man robbed in the Marketplace, so he buys him a drink instead, and the man goes home warm and still robbed. His uncle Volen runs the stills now and throws a party at the Stormhaven manor once a month for a few guests picked on a whim, and the invitation is the only storm in Stormreach anyone prays for. I asked a man at the tap what he was drinking to. He gave it careful thought, said it would come to him, and raised the cup anyway."
]},
{ id:"song-and-calling", cat:"desire", place:"harbor-docks", title:"The Song and the Calling", body:[
"A priest of the Sovereign Host who hears it calls it Dol Arrah. A keeper of the Silver Flame calls it the Voice of the Flame. A kalashtar calls it a herald of il-Yannah, the light of the age to come. A shifter off the Sarlonan tundra calls it by her word for wind. A pickpocket in the Harbor has no word for it at all; he stops in the middle of a lift with tears running into his collar, and the mark walks off with his own purse. All of them hear a choir that swells when others who hear it are near, and sooner or later all of them buy passage to Stormreach.",
"Others hear a whisper at the edge of sleep, full of a continent they have never seen and a power asleep inside it. They buy passage too.",
"Both kinds of passenger are met at the foot of the gangplank, and in this town that is never good news. The choir's people are met by Gilead Rhoy, once a Brelish noble, who floats a hand's breadth above the cobbles on a small carpet with a blanket over his legs, the warforged Boorne at one shoulder and the kalashtar Leahtari at the other. The whisper's people are met, usually inside the hour, by men and women who give no names and take them below the city. Gilead lost the use of his legs down there in his first days ashore, in a room the Cabal of Shadows kept him in until his own people broke him out. He has never said what was done to him. He says the whisper is a twisted echo of the Song. Below the city they say the Song is a twisted echo of the whisper.",
"I am told the two sides have compared, in private, what they hear, and that the descriptions agree. Mornings, the man on the carpet and the people from below wait on the quay a few yards apart, like two fences waiting on the same cargo. When a passenger comes down the plank with that look on his face, they both step forward."
]},
{ id:"kreldo", cat:"signs", place:"cross-hovel", title:"Kreldo", body:[
"Going into Xen'drik without reading the omens is bad luck, and in this town bad luck is a debt that someone else holds the paper on. The city is full of veiled stalls and curtained back rooms thick with incense, and most of the fortune-tellers in them work for the Hollow Shards. They predict success, because success tips well. A rival who wants your expedition to fail can pay them to predict otherwise, and the Shards will take his coin and yours on the same afternoon.",
"In a hovel in Cross there is one they do not own. He is a hulking hobgoblin who has plainly seen twenty winters and insists he is eight. He plays on the floor with a wooden lightning rail and a box of toy monsters, chattering to them. A crotchety old half-orc named Skurga, his nurse or his wife depending on who is telling it, takes your coin at the door. Bring him a good wooden toy and he will talk to the spirits for you. His eyes roll back, his nose bleeds, and a howl comes out of him that belongs to no child. Then he tells you what will happen, and it does. I watched a mercenary captain come out of that hovel white to the lips, walk straight across Cross to the nearest curtained stall, and pay a Shard to tell him something else."
]},
{ id:"black-freighter", cat:"rigging", place:"black-freighter", title:"The Black Freighter", body:[
"The best-loved tavern in Southwatch is a ship that has never sailed. It sits in the river on a hull of green stone the thri-kreen left behind for a purpose long forgotten, close to indestructible, and floating when by rights it ought to sink. You come aboard by walking a plank. At dawn, when the bar closes, the plank comes in, and whoever is still aboard is staying.",
"Inside, the food is what Karrnathi laborers eat, the drink is cheap and steady, and the air is thick with verse, a sea shanty at one table and a slow ballad of the homeland at the next. The proprietor calls himself Captain Smargat. He wears a tricorn hat and greets every face with the same advice, to be sure it is in the right establishment. Regulars will tell you, after a few, that the face under the tricorn is not always the face that was under it last night. Agents of the Emerald Claw drink there in the open beside soldiers of the Swords of Karrn, and in the corner a prematurely grey man in white linen sits with a raven on his shoulder and pays for nothing. The story goes that the Freighter could slip its mooring and sail downriver any night it liked. It cannot. It is fixed to the riverbed. Its floor has hatches that open onto the ruins under the city, and on busy nights more people leave through them than ever came up the plank."
]},
{ id:"night-tide", cat:"trade", place:"harbor-docks", title:"The Night Tide", body:[
"Stormreach has a second market, and it has no tent. It takes its name from the days when smugglers came in on the night tide, it is bigger than the black markets of cities three times the size, and almost everyone in the city uses it for something before breakfast.",
"Its tables are never in the same place twice. From them, in a single week, you could have bought Brelish sausages; Aundairian wine; a Siberys shard the size of a gnome's head; a dagger used in blood rites twenty thousand years ago; a potion from Tyrl Gol Garin, the bespectacled gnome who keeps the Pestle and who may, on any given day, poison one for his own amusement; a sword from a dark-haired dwarf missing more fingers than he has left; and anything at all from a fat man with his hair slicked back in whale grease, who calls himself an entrepreneur and cheats on every weight.",
"A relic sold openly in Stormreach pays four parts in ten to the Storm Lords, unless its seller holds a letter of marque, and the rule is applied loosely. The Night Tide pays the Storm Lords nothing. It pays a tithe, through a mouthpiece in each gang, to someone the fences call the Spider, and the Spider, as each fence knows and none will say, has a seat among the Storm Lords and runs the city's courts. The tables fold at first light. By the time the bazaar raises its red cloth the Night Tide is a row of empty crates on the quay with gulls standing on them."
]},
{ id:"katanavash", cat:"eyes", place:"riedran-consulate", title:"The Riedran Consulate", body:[
"A short walk from the Wavecrest Tavern lives the consul of Riedra, Lord Katanavash, a handsome man with a mediator's manners. He is one of the Inspired, his mind shared with a spirit out of the realm of dreams. Riedran sailors who put in here find every terrible story they were ever told about the humans beyond their borders waiting on the quay, and they stay aboard. The consul goes ashore. He walks the city in plain clothes and lays his hands on things, old stones, doorposts, the relics explorers drag back, and reads what has happened to them. He will pay well to hold something from the jungle for an hour. It is said he prefers the past he finds in such things to the present he walks through to reach them, and in Stormreach it is hard to blame him.",
"On the days he would rather not be seen, his assistant goes out in his place, wearing his face. She is a changeling named Hope, devoted to the spirit that lives in him, and she believes she will be reborn one day as a vessel for its kind. All twelve of his household know it, the four guards, the six servants, the two clerks, and say nothing. Hope shakes the right hands and smiles at the right merchants, and she is better at it than he is. There are mornings when the consul of Riedra is shaking hands in the Marketplace and kneeling in the Harbor with his palm flat on a giant's doorstep, with his eyes closed."
]},
{ id:"iron-watch", cat:"names", place:"iron-watch", title:"The Watch House", body:[
"In Forgelight there is a house where nobody sleeps. Its tenants are warforged, built for the Last War, who once served a Brelish commander that spent them like arrows. After seeing one too many comrades shattered, a warforged named Steeljack took his squad, commandeered a boat, and sailed to Stormreach. Lady Paulo Omaren hired them as a counterweight to the Amanatus, who train the Guard and own most of it, and they became the Iron Watch, called out when the Guard meets something it cannot handle or has been paid not to. They take no bribes, which makes them the strangest law in the city. They draw no wages either. The gold goes toward a home for their kind.",
"They name their ranks for what they are made of. Wooden Guard. Stone Guard. Iron Guard. Steel Guard. Adamant. Their three Adamants are Steeljack, Smith and Lathe, and of the third it is said that no house in Khorvaire built him. He walked up out of the dungeons under the city some time after the Watch was founded, sat down among them, and began teaching the youngest to knit their own wood back together by thinking about it. The Watch has never asked him what he was doing down there, or for how long.",
"At night they stand awake in the street outside their house and watch the windows of the city go dark one at a time. A dockworker told me he would rather have the Karrnathi wolves, because those at least were human. When I passed this on to Steeljack, he allowed that the dockworker was right about the Karrns, and asked me which window was mine, so that they would know when I was asleep."
]},
{ id:"fleas", cat:"dead", place:"the-sloths", title:"The Fleas", body:[
"You will meet the Fleas inside an hour of landing; they are the city's welcome. Six or eight children close around you, begging for coppers and sweets, crying, laughing, tugging at your sleeve, and when they scatter your purse goes with one of them. They come in every race. There are about forty, with dens in the Sloths, the Harbor and Locksmith Square, reached through holes too small for you. In a street fight they dart in and strip the fallen while the fighters are still busy, and the winners never learn what they won.",
"Their boss is a boy of ten, blackened and bloated, who drowned in the harbor longer ago than anyone can date. Most believe a guard pushed him, and that his bones are still out there, drifting. He has never told his name. The children call him Drifter. He hates grown people, and when a Flea grows up he throws them out, so the gang never ages and its faces are always new. Sometimes on the docks you will see a young man watching a ring of children close around a merchant, watching very carefully and helping neither side. He is one of the ones who were thrown out. He will not tell you what it was like. He stands there until the ring breaks and the merchant pats his coat, and then he looks out at the water, the way a man looks at a door he used to have the key to."
]},
{ id:"shrouds", cat:"dead", place:"twilight-streets", title:"The Shrouds", body:[
"In the red hour before the lamps are lit, a funeral comes down the street with no body in it. The mourners are wrapped in crimson grave-cloth, and where the cloth has slipped their faces are rotten. A shopkeeper who would haggle with any other extortionist in the city sets his coins on the sill and steps back from the window. Living elves cut many of those faces themselves, with scalpels, and blacken the cuts with poison. The rest did not need cutting.",
"At the head walks Whisper, priestess of the Keeper, nearly seven feet tall and long in the limb, in wrappings that smell of perfume and of what the perfume is for. Her brother walks at her side. He was killed in a street fight and came back, and now he goes veiled from crown to foot, and the shopkeepers believe the face under the veil kills whoever sees it, so they keep their eyes on the coins. The Guard keeps its eyes elsewhere. Whisper's tithe goes where the other gangs' tithes go, and the Spider looks after her own.",
"House Tharashk sent its best inquisitive, Shurya d'Velderan, who bills a hundred gold and fifty more by the hour, to look at one of their killings. Her report said it was a single step in a ritual that might take a century to finish, and that she could not tell which step. The Shrouds have been destroyed several times in fifty years. Each time, after a season or two, the funeral has come back up out of the tunnels at the red hour, and the next killing has been the next step, counted from where the last one left off."
]},
{ id:"wyrm-ascendant", cat:"sky", place:"sovereign-host", title:"The Temple of the Wyrm Ascendant", body:[
"The Sovereign Host has a temple in Forgelight, built before the city had a Temple district, and in it the gods have wings. The mosaics show them breathing fire on fiends. Dol Dorn over the side altar has a soldier's shield and a long scaled neck, and the mercenaries who kneel to him before a contract never look up far enough to notice. Over the gate hangs the eight-pointed star, the same as anywhere.",
"The priests teach that the greatest dragons rose into the heavens and became the gods, and that the faithful may rise after them. A dragon does not rise without its hoard. In this congregation, then, the devout keep accounts, a fat purse is a sign of grace, and a miracle is priced by the weight of the wound. The deacon who blessed my coins weighed them first in his palm, kindly, and assured me I was in no danger of heaven yet.",
"The high priest, Maru Sakhesh, is cruel and very good at raising money. Adventurers killed him last year. House Jorasco raised him for a fee, which the temple paid without complaint and entered in its books as an investment, and he has looked hard at strangers ever since. The young priestess Tania Drake, who wants his chair, has been looking just as hard at him. Under the floor, the congregation believes, lies a vault holding two centuries of tithes, dragonscale armor stripped from dead Seren barbarians, and a single scale plucked from the hide of Aureon, all guarded by curses. The faithful mean to climb. Every coin they give is carried down the stairs."
]},
{ id:"the-horizon", cat:"storms", place:"southwatch-edge", title:"The Southern Horizon", body:[
"Stand at the edge of Southwatch and turn slowly.",
"To the south, where the jungle meets the sky, a black cloud sits on the horizon and does not move. Sailors steer by it. Inside it, on thunderheads, there is said to be a citadel, and in the citadel a roc grown old and wise who calls himself immortal and has decided that Stormreach is a blight on the land, to be ground back into rubble. The giants of the Tents hold him off with rites and sacrifices. The city does not ask which ones.",
"Inland, less than a day's flight for a dragon, a single sandstone spire stands in a storm that never ends. The blue dragon who lives there is the son of the dragon Delera Omaren killed when the city was a pirate camp. He was young then. He has had a long time to think about it.",
"Out to sea, a kraken has begun to stir, and the sahuagin have started saying its name. It has never yet touched a Lyrandar ship, and Lyrandar has never been asked why.",
"Somewhere in the interior the fire giants of the Basalt Towers, who rained stars on this city in 946 and left craters you can still stand in, are rebuilding. Behind you, as you finish turning, is the city, with its lamps coming on and every head in its streets bent over a ledger, a purse, a hand of cards."
]},
{ id:"blood-devil", cat:"oaths", place:"marketplace-oath", title:"The Blood Devil", body:[
"Pirates did not trust ink. The only promise a pirate kept was one sworn in blood, and the habit has outlived the pirates. In Stormreach merchants seal a contract by cutting their palms and pressing them together. Brothers swear kinship that way, and lovers on the piers at Pirate's Moon, and partners over a map before they go into the jungle to find out which of them was lying.",
"Everyone knows what comes for someone who breaks that kind of oath. A hulking red thing, with a mane soaked in gore and a mouth lined with leeches, which drinks every drop of his blood, since he plainly did not value it much. No one claims to have seen it. Everyone knows someone who knew someone who was found white and empty in an alley the week after a deal went bad.",
"The Storm Lords enforce what profits them. The Guard enforces what it is paid to. Everything else promised in this city, loans between neighbors, debts of honor, the shares of a find split at a jungle campfire, is kept by a creature that may not exist and cannot be bought, and on the whole it is kept well. I shook hands on a small loan in Cross. The man looked at my unscarred palm with pity and named a higher rate."
]},
{ id:"ivory-horn", cat:"oaths", place:"red-ring", title:"The Ivory Horn", body:[
"The best fighters in the Red Ring sign themselves to it for life. They swear a blood oath to a stablemaster, and they wear his brand and an iron collar until they die. You are god and slave both, one of them told me. A dog on a leash in the street. The center of the world on the sand.",
"Three stablemasters sit on the Blood Council. Malketh Zolark wears only grey and black and hates color. His family built the Ring three centuries ago, more than thirty championship belts hang over his hearth, and the stable came to him young, when his father died in circumstances people discuss only in whispers and never in front of him. Ralzina Voraal arrived three years ago with old money clinging to her, wears silk in green and red, and bought a failing stable from an old master who now works for her as its handler. She believes the sigil she burns into her fighters will deliver their souls to her when they die, so she sends them to die with some enthusiasm. The third is Kolos, a minotaur with crimson fur and polished horns. He was a slave on this sand. He won his freedom by breaking an ogre called Deathreaver who had not lost in two years, and kept winning until he had a seat at the same table as the men who had owned him. His stable owns no slaves and splits every purse.",
"He dresses the way the other two do, in a lord's finery. Up close the velvet is patched and the buttons are missing, because his money goes to his fighters. On his forearm he leaves bare the brand of a stable that no longer exists, and he shows it to anyone who asks and to many who do not. The crowds who come to watch men sign their lives away cheer him louder than anyone, and then go to the window and put their money on Zolark."
]},
{ id:"travelers-inn", cat:"hidden", place:"travelers-inn", title:"The Traveler's Inn", body:[
"One of the inns of Stormreach is a temple, and nothing about it says so. It has a sign, a common room, a hearth, beds of the usual quality, and staff who are pleasant to you. The other guests are sailors, scholars and adventurers. The god it serves is the Traveler, the one with many faces, and its staff and many of its regulars have given themselves to changing the life of anyone who walks through the door.",
"You will not notice it start. A conversation at the next table turns out to be about you. An old map turns up in a crack between the floorboards of your room. A long-lost relative takes the room across the hall. A merchant offers you an object of unexplained power at a price that is almost reasonable. Or, a few days after you settle up and leave, the Guard kicks in your door over a terrible crime you did not commit, and the witnesses are people you remember from breakfast.",
"Ask an innkeeper which inn it is and you will be told the name of a rival. There is a proverb from Sarlona about the Traveler's gifts, and each people in the harbor has its own version of it. You have slept somewhere since you landed. Think about the faces in the common room this morning, and whether one of them looked at you a moment longer than it needed to, and whether, since then, you have felt any different."
]}
];

// Vignettes, part C
const CITY_EXTRA = [
{ id:"blackbriar", cat:"hidden", place:"blackbriar", title:"Blackbriar", body:[
"North of the city along the coast lies a field of black briar that cannot be crossed. It is waist-high at the road and higher as you go in, until it closes over your head, and then, all at once, it stops, and you are standing in grassy hills with a manor at their center and a dirt path leading to it. That is, if House Vadalis wants you there. The ranch is the largest holding of any house in Xen'drik, and the briar grows and shrinks at the housemaster's whim. A servant meets visitors at the road with grave warnings about straying. Those the house has not invited walk into the thorns and come out somewhere else, scratched and puzzled, with nothing to show for it.",
"The founder, Mundir d'Vadalis, was trampled to death by one of his own animals not long after Thronehold. Few ask how. His daughter Khepra is one of the few. She expected the house; it went instead to Tyris d'Vadalis, a breedmaster sent away from Khorvaire over unauthorized experiments. The hands are sworn to secrecy and will hint, at most, that they were hired to bring strange beasts out of the interior. I stood at the edge of the field one evening with no wind blowing and watched the thorns nearest the road lean slowly toward me, and then, having apparently considered me, lean away."
]}
];

// Night frames: the traveler and the Emperor
const FRAMES = [
{ title:"The First Night", body:[
"On the cliffs above Stormreach harbor stands a giant of stone with its palms pressed together and a column of light rising out of its hands. It faces the sea. It has faced the sea for forty thousand years, and it has never once seen the city at its back.",
"I climbed up to it my first night ashore because it was the only place in Stormreach where a stranger could sit down without being robbed, and because I had nobody else to report to, I told it what I had seen that day. I was most of the way through when the stone answered. The voice came up through the rock like the tide working in the caves under the Wavecrest, and each word took as long as a wave to finish.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You are very small, and you came from behind me. Nothing comes from behind me. Is my city still there?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> There's a city behind you, Majesty. Whether it's still yours is another question. Give me a season and I'll find out who it belongs to now.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Tell it slowly. The sea has said the same thing to me for forty thousand years. I had forgotten anything else could be said."
]},
{ title:"The Second Night", body:[
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You spoke of rope. Of tents, and driftwood, and a ward where people buy the pieces of a dead country. My city was stone. Its stairs were cut for my feet, and each step was the height of your whole body. You are describing some other city.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> I'm describing the only one there is. Yours is underneath it, in pieces. The ropes are tied to it. The tents are pitched in its doorways. Your stairs are still there, Majesty. The tenants have cut smaller stairs into them.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Into my stairs.",
"The giant was quiet so long I thought it had closed the interview. The beam from its hands went on pouring into the clouds. Down in the harbor a bell was ringing, and somebody was singing in a language I didn't know, badly, the way people sing when they have just been paid.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> How small are the smaller stairs?"
]},
{ title:"The Third Night", body:[
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> I have been counting. Of every place you describe, you begin with what it lacks. A country. A name. A forecast anyone can trust.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> That's how Stormreach introduces itself. Other ports show you the palace first. Here they show you the hole where something used to be, and then whoever moved into it, and then the bill.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> There is a hole where I used to be. Who moved into it?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Pirates, Majesty. Then their grandchildren. Then everyone else.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> I do not know the word pirate. Is it a kind of giant?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> It's a kind of everyone."
]},
{ title:"The Fourth Night", body:[
"The fog came in thick that night, and the floating stones over Respite went into it one at a time, like lamps being put out.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> How long do your kind last?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Sixty years, if we're lucky. Seventy. In this town, a good deal less.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Since the light was put in my hands the shore has come toward me four times and gone back three. I have watched one island rise out of the sea and two go under it. Your whole life would pass while I decided whether a wave was large.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Then you'd better listen quickly, Majesty.",
"The light in the giant's hands flickered, as if something far down in the stone had found that funny. Then it asked me to tell it again about the woman with the rag and the bowl who cleans the stone heads, and I told it twice, and the second time it stopped me to ask what a pickled apple was."
]},
{ title:"The Fifth Night", body:[
"The wind came off the land that night, and with it the smell of the fires on the eastern cliffs, where the city was building something tall out of wicker.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> I smell burning. I remember fire coming down out of the sky. I remember the dragons. Are they burning us again?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Only the city burns you now, Majesty, once a year, in wicker, wearing dragon masks. Forgelight gets the week off and Molou's sells out of gin. They don't know whose effigy it is. If they did, they'd charge admission.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Then the dragons won the war and the festival both. They have taught you to dance at it.",
"I had no answer for that, so I told it about the Red Ring instead. It listened to the end, and then asked who had won, and when I didn't know, it went quiet a long time, the way a bookmaker goes quiet, working out the odds."
]},
{ title:"The Sixth Night", body:[
"It was Pirate's Moon. All evening the city had been walking out to the ends of the piers and throwing bread and rings and unsent letters into the harbor, and from the cliff we could hear each one go in.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> I've been wondering what you were set here to guard against. The scholars say the demons of the past. The fishers say the sea.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> I was set here to face whatever came. Nobody told me it would come from behind, on foot, a few at a time, with rope and hammers and songs. I watched the sea for an enemy for forty thousand years. The enemy moved in behind me and opened a tavern.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Several taverns, Majesty. One of them floats.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Tell me about the one that floats.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> I haven't been aboard yet. A man who drinks there tells me there's a trick to the plank. I'll go tomorrow and report.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You say tomorrow as though it were a small thing."
]},
{ title:"The Seventh Night", body:[
"I had been aboard the Black Freighter, as promised, and I told the giant about the plank drawn in at dawn and the hatches in the floor. It asked where the hatches led. Before I could answer it said that it did not want to know, and the beam in its hands narrowed, the way an eye narrows.",
"Then it wanted to understand the city by its rules, the way a king learns a game by its pieces. I told it Stormreach has one law, which is to make no trouble for the Storm Lords. Everything else is permitted, or for sale.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Then I need only understand the five lords, and I will understand the city.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> They play a card game here called Three-Dragon Ante. Meeting a guard, they say, is a blind draw: one time the platinum dragon, the next the Fool. The lords are the same deck. The Harbor Lord spends his nights at a brothel and his days asleep. Each of the others is said to have a second face. One of them, the rumor goes, is a fiend who has watched this coast for fifty thousand years and plays at politics to pass the time.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Fifty thousand. Then he was here before me.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Then he's been behind you the whole time.",
"Deep in the cliff there was a sound I had not heard before, like a millstone starting to turn. Grit ran down the statue's shoulders in a thin stream. The beam swung a hand's width off true, wavered, and came back. Down in the harbor a few lamps went on in windows, as if people had felt something through their beds. The giant said nothing else that night, and I didn't push it. You learn not to push a client who has just found out who his partner is."
]},
{ title:"The Eighth Night", body:[
"A hard storm came in, and I sat in the lee of the giant's knee with the rain going past me sideways. The lightning found Falconer's Spire twice, and the giant not at all. In the Dinghy, I knew, someone was already chalking it on the beam.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> There's an old hill giant who sells fruit and herbs at a tent by the city gate. His grandmother came to the pirates when they first landed and warned them that the land was cursed. They laughed at her. He still repeats her warning to anyone who buys from him. They still laugh. He still takes their money.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> My people. However small they have become. What will they do when the city falls?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Nothing to stop it. They've given up on saving Stormreach. They watch it, they keep a record of its folly, and they mean to see that whatever it lets loose doesn't get past this coast.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Then they are my children. That is exactly what I am doing.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Is it, Majesty? You watch the sea. They watch the city.",
"The light in its hands dimmed and brightened, slowly, three times, like a long breath drawn and let go.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> That is a fine distinction, for something that will last seventy years."
]},
{ title:"The Ninth Night", body:[
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Tonight I'd like to tell you what's under the city. There are iron doors behind the Waterworks, and a guide who will take me through them.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Do not. I know what is beneath. I knew it when they carved me. The wizards of my city reached for it, and when they understood what they had reached, they set me on this cliff and turned me to the sea, so that whatever stood here would always be facing away from it.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Why away?",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Some things grow when they are looked at.",
"The light in its hands burned very steady that night, and very bright. Below us it was Shacklebreak. Bonfires were going in the Marketplace and masks in every street, and the noise came up the cliff in gusts. I left the undercity out. I told it about the bonfires instead, and a market, and a dwarf drumming the names of the dead, and the giant listened as though each of these were a stone being laid on top of something it did not want to wake."
]},
{ title:"The Tenth Night", body:[
"I went through the iron doors anyway, the morning after it told me not to. In my trade you don't take a client's word for what's in the cellar. I did not tell the giant. Some things you keep out of the report, and this was the first time I had ever done it to spare the client rather than myself.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You have described a city of rope tied to my bones and laid over a thing I was carved to ignore. Why does anyone stay?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> I asked. Most couldn't go anywhere else. Some were exiled. Some are waiting for the war to start again. Some are here for one more expedition, and have been for thirty years.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> And you will go. Your kind always goes.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> At the end of the season, Majesty. The packet sails on the first fair wind Lyrandar can be paid to arrange.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> Then before you go, tell me the last of it, and tell me what you would do in my place. I have stood in this place for forty thousand years, and no one has ever told me what they would do in it."
]}
];

const EPILOGUE = { title:"Before Dawn", body:[
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You have told me the last of it. Now tell me the other thing. What would you do in my place?",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Turn around, Majesty.",
"<span class=\"v\">The Emperor:</span> You heard what I told you. Some things grow when they are looked at.",
"<span class=\"v\">The traveler:</span> Then look at the roofs. Whatever's under the city, you'd have to see it through the city, and the city is in the way. Two hundred years of it, rope and laundry and stilts, people who owe each other money and keep their oaths in blood because they don't trust ink. They weren't hired to stand there. They're standing there anyway, and nothing older than they are has ever looked at them.",
"The giant didn't answer. Below us the first boats were going out. The beam in its hands went pale against the sky, the way it does every morning. Then came the sound I had heard only once before, the millstone deep in the cliff, very slow, and a thin stream of grit ran down the great shoulders and scattered in the grass. In that grey light I would swear, and have sworn since in every port where someone has bought me a drink to hear it, that the stone head had turned, a very little, toward the city."
]};
